# Batch 37 (2026-11-24): Slavic, Baltic, Carpathian and Central/Eastern European myth, history, forgeries and anomalies; Soviet-era secrets; steppe
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: Slavic gods, spirits and sanctuaries
E+=[
X('perun-thunder-god','Perun: the Slavic thunder god of the oak and the axe',M,'myth-legend',2,980,['textual','archaeological','comparative mythology'],
 "Perun was the chief god of the pre-Christian Slavs: a god of the sky, thunder, lightning, rain, war and oak trees, often linked to the axe and the eagle. Very little was written down by Slavs themselves before Christianisation, so he is known mainly from outsiders and Christian clerics. The 6th-century Byzantine historian Procopius described a Slavic god of lightning who was 'lord of all'; the Russian Primary Chronicle records that Kyivan Rus' princes swore treaties with the Byzantines by Perun (907, 945, 971) and that Prince Vladimir set up an idol of Perun with a silver head and golden moustache on a hill in Kyiv in 980, before converting to Christianity in 988 and having it thrown into the Dnieper. Place names, oaths, folk sayings and the later cult of the prophet Elijah (Ilya), who took over his thunder role in folk belief, all preserve traces of him. Linguists link his name to the Lithuanian thunder god Perkūnas and to a reconstructed Proto-Indo-European storm god.",
 [('Reconstructed storm-god myth','In the 1970s Vyacheslav Ivanov and Vladimir Toporov reconstructed a "basic myth" in which the thunder god Perun pursues and strikes his serpent-like rival Veles, who hides in trees, stones and water; parallels are drawn with Thor and the Midgard serpent and Indra and Vritra. This reconstruction is influential but partly hypothetical, pieced together from folklore and comparison rather than a surviving Slavic text.'),
  ('Sceptical view','Some scholars caution that medieval Christian writers may have shaped, simplified or invented parts of the "Slavic pantheon", so how organised and uniform Perun\'s cult was across all Slavic peoples is debated.'),
  ('Modern revival','Perun is honoured today by Rodnovers (Slavic Native Faith), a modern religious movement; their rituals are a 20th-21st century reconstruction rather than unbroken tradition.')],
 ['wiki:Perun','wiki:Slavic_paganism','url:https://www.britannica.com/topic/Perun|Encyclopaedia Britannica: Perun|encyclopedia'],img='File:Perun in Bitca.jpg',dateNote='Cult attested c. 6th-10th centuries; Kyiv idol set up 980 and removed after 988'),
X('veles-serpent-god','Veles (Volos): god of cattle, wealth and the underworld',M,'myth-legend',3,907,['textual','comparative mythology','folklore'],
 "Veles, also called Volos, was a Slavic god described in the Russian Primary Chronicle as the 'god of cattle' (skotiy bog); Kyivan Rus' swore by him alongside Perun in their treaties with Byzantium. Cattle were a measure of wealth, so he was also associated with riches and trade, and in later folklore with the underworld, the dead, magic, music and poetry (the 12th-century Tale of Igor's Campaign calls the bard Boyan 'grandson of Veles'). After Christianisation many of his functions passed to Saint Blaise (Vlasiy), protector of cattle, and some place names may preserve his name.",
 [('Thunder god\'s adversary','In the reconstruction by Ivanov and Toporov, Veles is the serpent or dragon-like opponent of Perun, stealing cattle (or Perun\'s wife) and being struck down by lightning, an annual cosmic battle that brings rain. This is a scholarly hypothesis built from folklore, not a myth preserved in a single ancient text.'),
  ('Same or different gods','Scholars debate whether Veles and Volos were originally one god or two, and how far the chthonic "underworld" role is genuinely ancient rather than a later interpretation.'),
  ('Forgery link','The 20th-century "Book of Veles" uses his name but is regarded by scholars as a forgery and is not evidence for his cult (see its own entry).')],
 ['wiki:Veles_(god)','wiki:Slavic_paganism'],img='File:Veles by Andrey Shishkin.jpg'),
X('mokosh','Mokosh: the only goddess in Prince Vladimir\'s pantheon',M,'myth-legend',3,980,['textual','folklore'],
 "Mokosh is the only female deity named in the Russian Primary Chronicle's list of idols that Prince Vladimir of Kyiv set up in 980 (with Perun, Khors, Dazhbog, Stribog and Simargl). Medieval Christian sermons against paganism mention her alongside vilas (nymphs) but do not describe her. Later East Slavic folklore links a figure called Mokosh or Mokusha with spinning, sheep-shearing, women's work, moisture and the earth, and Saint Paraskeva Friday seems to have absorbed some of these customs.",
 [('Great mother goddess','Many modern writers interpret Mokosh as a mother-earth or fertility goddess, perhaps related to "Mother Moist Earth" (Mat Syra Zemlya); this is plausible but largely reconstructed from later folk practice.'),
  ('Finnic origin','Some linguists suggest her name may be borrowed from a Finnic people (the Moksha), which would make her worship a sign of cultural mixing in early Rus\'; this is a debated hypothesis.'),
  ('Caution','Because no narrative myth about her survives, every modern description is an interpretation; scholars disagree over how important she really was.')],
 ['wiki:Mokosh','wiki:Slavic_paganism'],img='Mokosh',dateNote='Named in the Primary Chronicle entry for 980'),
X('rusalka','Rusalki: the drowned water maidens of Slavic folklore',M,'myth-legend',3,1800,['folklore','ethnographic'],
 "In Slavic folklore rusalki are female spirits associated with water, fields and forests, often thought to be the souls of girls or young women who died before marriage, by drowning or by suicide, or unbaptised children. In 19th-century ethnographic records from Russia, Ukraine and Belarus they appear with long loose hair, sitting in birch trees or by rivers, tickling or luring men to their deaths, especially during 'Rusalka Week' in early summer around Trinity (Pentecost), when villagers performed rituals to see them off. Earlier medieval sources mention a festival called rusalii, a name probably derived from the Roman spring festival of roses (Rosalia).",
 [('Fertility spirits','Some folklorists argue rusalki were originally benign spirits who brought moisture and fertility to fields, and became sinister only later under Christian influence; others think the "unquiet dead" idea was central from the start.'),
  ('Shared motif','The idea of dangerous water women who lure people to drown appears widely, for example the Germanic Lorelei and nixies, Greek sirens and African Mami Wata traditions; whether these spring from shared roots or similar human fears is open.'),
  ('Literal belief','Within traditional village belief rusalki were real beings to be avoided and appeased; there is no physical evidence for them.')],
 ['wiki:Rusalka'],img='Rusalka',dateNote='Recorded mainly in 18th-19th century ethnography; the rusalii festival is attested in medieval sources'),
X('domovoy','The domovoy: the house spirit behind the stove',M,'myth-legend',3,1800,['folklore','ethnographic'],
 "The domovoy (Russian; Ukrainian domovyk, Polish domowik) is the household spirit of East and West Slavic folklore, usually imagined as a small, hairy old man living behind the stove or under the threshold. If treated well, with offerings of bread, porridge or milk, he protected the family and livestock; if neglected or offended he made noises, tangled horses' manes or pinched sleepers. When a family moved house they would carry embers from the old stove, or a slipper, to invite him along. Folklorists recorded these beliefs widely in the 19th and early 20th centuries.",
 [('Ancestor cult','Many scholars see the domovoy as a survival of ancestor worship, the founder of the household watching over descendants; this is a widely held but not provable interpretation.'),
  ('Parallels','Similar household spirits include the Scandinavian tomte/nisse, the Scottish brownie and the German kobold; a common European pattern of propitiating a house spirit is often suggested.'),
  ('Explanations of the uncanny','Sceptics see beliefs like the domovoy as a way of explaining household noises, sleep paralysis ("being pressed" at night) and luck with livestock.')],
 ['wiki:Domovoy'],img='Domovoy'),
X('leshy','The leshy: lord of the Slavic forest',M,'myth-legend',3,1800,['folklore','ethnographic'],
 "The leshy (leshiy) is the forest spirit of Slavic folklore, master of the woods and its animals. He was described as a shapeshifter who could appear as tall as the trees or as small as a blade of grass, often with hair and beard of moss or leaves, wearing his clothes inside out and his shoes on the wrong feet. He led travellers astray, made them walk in circles, and imitated familiar voices; to escape, peasants turned their own clothes inside out. Hunters and herdsmen made pacts or left offerings with him for success. Many such accounts were collected by 19th-century ethnographers.",
 [('Personification of nature','Folklorists commonly explain the leshy as a personification of the forest\'s dangers and the experience of getting lost in large northern woodlands.'),
  ('Relation to pagan gods','Some scholars connect him with a pre-Christian god of forests or hunting (sometimes the West Slavic Porewit is suggested); this link is speculative.'),
  ('Living belief','Similar forest guardians appear worldwide, such as the Brazilian curupira (feet turned backwards) and the Filipino tikbalang; within folk belief he was a real being, though there is no physical evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Leshy'],img='Leshy'),
X('kupala-night','Kupala Night: midsummer fires and the fern that blooms once a year',M,'myth-legend',3,1500,['folklore','textual','ethnographic'],
 "Kupala Night (Ivan Kupala) is a midsummer festival celebrated by many Slavs, traditionally on the night of 23-24 June (or 6-7 July in the Julian calendar), merged with the Christian feast of Saint John the Baptist. Customs include jumping over bonfires, girls floating flower wreaths on rivers to divine their future marriage, bathing in rivers and dew, and searching the forest at midnight for the 'fern flower', said to bloom only on that night and to grant luck, wealth or the ability to understand animals and find hidden treasure. Church writers condemned the festivities from at least the 16th century.",
 [('Pre-Christian solstice rite','Most scholars see Kupala as a continuation of pre-Christian summer-solstice rites of fire, water and fertility, Christianised as St John\'s Eve; parallels include Midsummer in Scandinavia and the Baltic Joninės/Jāņi.'),
  ('A god called Kupala?','Older writers spoke of a pagan god Kupala; many modern scholars think "Kupala" simply comes from a word for bathing and that the deity is a later invention.'),
  ('The fern flower','Botanically, ferns reproduce by spores and do not flower, so the fern flower is regarded as purely legendary; some have suggested glowing fungi or fireflies as inspiration (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:Kupala_Night'],img='Kupala Night'),
X('morana-marzanna','Morana (Marzanna): drowning the winter goddess',M,'myth-legend',3,1420,['folklore','textual','ethnographic'],
 "Morana (Czech, Slovak, Slovene), Marzanna (Polish) or Mara is a Slavic figure associated with winter, death and seasonal rebirth. In a spring custom still practised in Poland, Czechia, Slovakia and elsewhere, people make a straw effigy, dress it in women's clothes, carry it out of the village and burn it or throw it into a river, symbolically ending winter. Church synods tried to ban the custom: the Synod of Poznań in 1420 and other 15th-century church records already describe the drowning of the effigy, and the 15th-century chronicler Jan Długosz mentioned Marzanna as a Polish goddess.",
 [('Ancient goddess of death','Many accounts treat Morana as an ancient Slavic goddess of death and winter, paired with spring figures; scholars debate whether she was truly a goddess or a personified ritual effigy given divine status by later writers.'),
  ('Shared European rite','Comparable "carrying out death" or "burning winter" effigy rituals exist across Europe, which some see as a common agrarian tradition.'),
  ('Folk etymology','Her name is linked to Slavic roots for death (mor) and nightmare (mara), but etymologies are debated.')],
 ['wiki:Morana_(goddess)'],img='Morana (goddess)',dateNote='Custom attested by the 15th century; still practised today'),
X('zbruch-idol','The Zbruch Idol: a four-faced pillar from a dried-up river',H,'disputed',2,900,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Zbruch Idol is a 2.57 m tall limestone pillar with four faces under a single tall hat, carved with figures in three tiers that are often read as heavens, earth and underworld. It was found in August 1848, during a drought, in the Zbruch River near the village of Lychkivtsi (then in Austrian Galicia, now western Ukraine), and has been in the Archaeological Museum of Kraków since 1968. It is usually dated to the 9th-10th centuries and is one of very few surviving monuments presented as pre-Christian Slavic religious art. Excavations in the 1980s found sanctuaries on the nearby Bohit hill, where it may once have stood.",
 [('Which god?','It was long called "Światowid" after the four-headed god Svantevit of Arkona; scholars now think this identification is mistaken and suggest it shows several gods (Perun, Mokosh and others) or a cosmological scheme. These readings remain interpretations.'),
  ('19th-century fake?','In 2011 Aleksey Komar and Natalia Khamaiko proposed that the pillar is a Romantic-era fake, possibly made for the landowner Tymon Zaborowski and inspired by Saxo Grammaticus\'s description of Svantevit\'s statue; others defend its medieval date. The question is open.'),
  ('Non-Slavic origin','A minority view connects the carving style to steppe (Turkic) stone statues rather than to Slavic religion.')],
 ['wiki:Zbruch_Idol'],img='Zbruch Idol',statusNote='Object real; date and meaning disputed',dateNote='Usually dated 9th-10th century; found 1848'),
X('arkona-svantevit','Arkona: the last great temple of Svantevit (destroyed 1168)',H,'confirmed',2,1168,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Cape Arkona on the island of Rügen (now in Germany) held the fortified temple of Svantevit (Svetovit), chief god of the Slavic Rani tribe. The Danish chronicler Saxo Grammaticus described a wooden temple with a huge statue with four heads, holding a drinking horn whose contents were used to forecast the harvest, a sacred white horse used for oracles, and a temple treasury and armed retinue of 300. In 1168 King Valdemar I of Denmark and Bishop Absalon captured Arkona, had the statue chopped up and burned, and Rügen was Christianised. Coastal cliffs have since collapsed into the sea, so only part of the ramparts of the 'Jaromarsburg' survive; archaeological excavations have confirmed a fortified cult site.",
 [('How reliable is Saxo?','Saxo wrote to glorify the Danish crusade; scholars accept the destruction as historical but debate how far his vivid description of rites and idol is accurate or shaped by classical and Christian stereotypes.'),
  ('Christian saint behind the god?','Helmold of Bosau claimed Svantevit was a pagan corruption of Saint Vitus, whose relics had been brought to the region by missionaries; most modern scholars regard this as a medieval Christian polemic rather than a true origin.'),
  ('Lost to the sea','Because much of the site has eroded away, some evidence is permanently lost; claims about what lay beneath are necessarily speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Cape_Arkona','wiki:Svetovit'],img='File:Arkona-1860-Knoche-Fock.png'),
X('rethra','Rethra: the lost temple-city of the Lutici',H,'disputed',2,1068,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Rethra (Radogošč, Riedegost) was the main religious and political centre of the Redarians, part of the Lutician confederation of pagan Slavs between the Elbe and Oder, from the 10th to the 11th century. Thietmar of Merseburg (early 11th century) described a fortress with three horns and three gates beside a lake, containing a wooden temple with idols, a horse oracle and a boar that emerged from the lake before war. Adam of Bremen, about fifty years later, described a 'seat of idolatry' surrounded by a deep lake with nine gates, and reported that after the Slavic revolt of 1066 the head of the captured Bishop John of Mecklenburg was offered there. The last record is the Annals of Augsburg for 1068, describing its capture by Bishop Burchard of Halberstadt, who rode away on its sacred horse.",
 [('Where was it?','Its location has never been confirmed. The most widely favoured area is the islands and marshes of the Lieps and Tollense lakes near Neubrandenburg; earlier proposals such as Feldberg and Wanzka Abbey have been rejected.'),
  ('One place or two?','Some historians argue Thietmar and Adam may not even be describing the same site, or the same god (Thietmar names Svarožič, Adam Redigast).'),
  ('Symbolic description','The numbers three and nine, the lake and the boar may be literary symbols linking the temple to the underworld rather than accurate reporting; how literally to read the chroniclers is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Rethra','wiki:Polabian_Slavs'],img='File:Lieps slawen rekonstruktion.jpg',statusNote='Existence attested in chronicles; location unknown',dateNote='Flourished 10th-11th century; last recorded 1068'),
]
# --- part 2: West and East Slavic legends, forgeries, Bohemia and Hungary
E+=[
X('wawel-dragon','The Wawel Dragon of Kraków and the "dragon bones" at the cathedral door',M,'myth-legend',2,700,['textual','folklore','palaeontological'],
 "In Polish legend a dragon (Smok Wawelski) lived in a cave beneath Wawel Hill in Kraków and devoured cattle and people until it was tricked into eating a carcass stuffed with burning sulphur. The oldest version, in the 13th-century chronicle of Bishop Wincenty Kadłubek, credits the sons of the legendary King Krak (Krakus); a 14th-century chronicle credits Krak himself; and Marcin Bielski's chronicle of 1597 gives the credit to a cobbler called Skuba, the version best known today. The 'Dragon's Den' (Smocza Jama) is a real limestone cave under the hill. Bones hanging on a chain at the entrance of Wawel Cathedral, brought there in medieval times as dragon remains, are now identified as bones of a whale and Pleistocene animals; local tradition says the world will end if they fall.",
 [('Fossils behind dragons','Like other "dragon bones" displayed in European churches, the Wawel bones show how fossil and large animal remains were interpreted as proof of dragons; some writers argue such finds helped inspire the legends themselves.'),
  ('Borrowed story','Scholars note the sulphur-bait trick closely resembles older tales, such as the Babylonian-Jewish story of Daniel killing a dragon with pitch, fat and hair, and a sulphur trick in the Syriac Alexander Romance, suggesting the plot travelled.'),
  ('Founding myth','The legend links the slaying of a monster to the founding of Kraków and of a royal line, a common pattern in dragon-slayer foundation stories across Europe and Asia.')],
 ['wiki:Wawel_Dragon','wiki:Krakus'],img='Wawel Dragon',dateNote='Set in legendary times before the 10th century; first written c. 1200'),
X('lech-czech-rus','Lech, Czech and Rus: three brothers and the white eagle',M,'myth-legend',3,600,['textual','folklore'],
 "A founding legend tells of three Slavic brothers who set out with their peoples: Rus went east, Čech (Czech) settled at the mountain Říp in Bohemia, and Lech travelled north, where he saw a white eagle guarding its nest against a red sunset, founded Gniezno (from gniazdo, 'nest') and took the eagle as his emblem, which remains Poland's coat of arms and the colours of its flag. Čech appears alone (as 'Bohemus') in Cosmas of Prague's chronicle of 1125; the Chronicle of Dalimil (1314) has him look out from Říp over a 'land of milk and honey'; the three brothers appear together first in the Chronicle of Greater Poland (late 13th-early 14th century).",
 [('Legend, not history','Historians treat the brothers as eponymous ancestors invented to explain the names of peoples (Lechia, Czechia, Rus\') and their kinship, a pattern found in many cultures, such as the sons of Noah in Genesis.'),
  ('Memory of migration','Some writers suggest the story preserves a distant memory of Slavic migrations in the 6th-7th centuries; the archaeological and genetic picture of these migrations is real, but the legend itself offers no independent evidence.'),
  ('National politics','The best-known version is Poland-centred, giving the eagle only to Lech; versions in different countries emphasise their own brother.')],
 ['wiki:Lech,_Czech,_and_Rus'],img='Lech, Czech, and Rus',dateNote='Set in the early Slavic migration period; written down 12th-14th centuries'),
X('libuse-prague','Libuše: the prophetess who foresaw Prague',M,'myth-legend',2,700,['textual','folklore'],
 "According to the Bohemian chronicle of Cosmas of Prague (c. 1120), Libuše was the youngest and wisest of three sisters and ruled the Czechs as judge and seer. When men resented being ruled by a woman she had a vision of a ploughman with one broken sandal; her envoys followed her horse to the village of Stadice, found Přemysl ploughing, and she married him, founding the Přemyslid dynasty, which actually ruled Bohemia until 1306. Later tradition has her stand on a cliff above the Vltava and prophesy 'a great city whose glory will touch the stars', the founding of Prague. Her story inspired Smetana's opera Libuše and is part of Czech national identity.",
 [('Myth with historical anchor','The Přemyslid dynasty is historical, but Libuše, Přemysl the Ploughman and the earliest rulers in Cosmas\'s list are regarded as legendary; there is no contemporary evidence for them.'),
  ('A misread source?','The musicologist Vladimír Karbusický suggested Cosmas may have built the names of the first Přemyslids from misreading an older text; this is a speculative but discussed theory.'),
  ('Woman ruler motif','Some scholars see in the legend memories of female religious authority among early Slavs, while others read it as a medieval tale justifying male rule (she is made to marry); both are interpretations.')],
 ['wiki:Libuše'],img='Libuše',dateNote='Legendary, set in the 8th century; first written c. 1120'),
X('biskupin','Biskupin: a Bronze-Iron Age fortified town used as a political weapon',H,'confirmed',2,-738,['archaeological','dendrochronology','radiocarbon'],
 "Biskupin in north-central Poland is a fortified settlement of the Lusatian culture built on a peninsula in a lake in the 8th century BC; tree-ring dating of its timbers places construction in the 8th century BC, commonly given as about 738-737 BC. It had a wooden rampart, a single gate, about 100 oak and pine log houses in neat rows and log-paved streets, home to perhaps 1,000 people. A schoolteacher, Walenty Szwajcer, noticed timbers in the lake in 1933, and Józef Kostrzewski and Zdzisław Rajewski excavated it. Waterlogging preserved the wood. It is now an open-air museum with reconstructions.",
 [('Proto-Slavic propaganda','In the 1930s Polish nationalists presented it as evidence that ancestors of Poles had defended themselves against Germanic invaders 2,700 years ago; when Germany occupied Poland in 1939 it was renamed "Urstädt" and Nazi archaeologists of the SS-Ahnenerbe dug there to claim a Germanic origin. Modern archaeologists say the Lusatian culture cannot be assigned to any modern nation.'),
  ('Flooded to destroy, preserved instead','Retreating German forces flooded the site in 1945; the water ironically helped preserve the timbers.'),
  ('Why abandoned?','It was abandoned within a few centuries, perhaps because of rising lake levels, a wetter climate or conflict; the cause is not settled.')],
 ['wiki:Biskupin'],img='Biskupin',dateNote='Built 8th century BC (commonly c. 738-737 BC, dendrochronology); discovered 1933',
 changed='1930s: hailed as a proto-Slavic "Polish Pompeii"; 1940-45: claimed as Germanic by the SS-Ahnenerbe; postwar: assigned to the Lusatian culture with no direct link to any modern nation.'),
X('book-of-veles','The Book of Veles: a "lost pagan chronicle" on wooden planks',H,'debunked',2,1919,['textual','linguistic'],
 "The Book of Veles is a text claimed to be an ancient Slavic religious history carved on 35 or more wooden planks. According to Yuri Mirolyubov, a White Army officer, Fyodor Isenbeck, found the planks in 1919 in a looted manor near Kharkiv, took them to Brussels, and Mirolyubov spent 15 years copying them before they were lost when Isenbeck died in 1941 during the German occupation. Only Mirolyubov's transcriptions and one photograph exist. They were published in the émigré magazine Zhar-Ptitsa in San Francisco from 1957 to 1959. Specialists in Slavic linguistics and history, including Boris Rybakov, Andrey Zaliznyak and Leo Klejn, regard it as a 20th-century forgery: its language is an inconsistent mixture that matches no known stage of any Slavic language.",
 [('Supporters','The text is popular among some Slavic neopagans and nationalist writers in Russia and Ukraine, who treat it as a genuine record of a glorious pre-Christian past; this view has no support in academic linguistics.'),
  ('Who forged it?','Most scholars point to Mirolyubov and his collaborators in the 1940s-50s; a less favoured view suggests the 19th-century forger Alexander Sulakadzev.'),
  ('Why it matters','It is often cited as an example of a "suppressed ancient book" story, alongside other forgeries discussed in this timeline.')],
 ['wiki:Book_of_Veles'],img='Book of Veles',dateNote='Allegedly found 1919, lost 1941, published 1957-59'),
X('tale-of-igor','The Tale of Igor\'s Campaign: medieval masterpiece or clever forgery?',H,'disputed',2,1185,['textual','linguistic'],
 "The Tale of Igor's Campaign (Slovo o polku Igoreve) is an Old East Slavic poem about the failed raid of Prince Igor Svyatoslavich of Novgorod-Seversky against the Polovtsians (Cumans) in 1185, which the Kyivan Chronicle independently records, including a solar eclipse on 1 May 1185. Its only known manuscript, said to be a 15th-16th century copy, was acquired around 1795 by Count Aleksei Musin-Pushkin from a monastery in Yaroslavl, published in 1800, and then destroyed in the Moscow fire of 1812, leaving only the printed edition and a copy made for Catherine the Great. It mixes Christian and pagan imagery (naming Veles, Dazhbog, Stribog and Khors) and is a foundational work of Russian and Ukrainian literature.",
 [('Forgery theories','Because the manuscript vanished, some scholars, including the French Slavist André Mazon (1940) and the Soviet historian Alexander Zimin (1960s), argued it was an 18th-century imitation based on the 15th-century Zadonshchina; Edward Keenan later proposed the Czech scholar Josef Dobrovský as author.'),
  ('Defence of authenticity','The linguist Andrey Zaliznyak (2004) argued that its subtle grammar matches 12th-century usage in ways no 18th-century forger could have known, much of it confirmed only by birch-bark letters found after 1951. Most scholars now accept it as genuine.'),
  ('Compilation view','A minority suggests it is genuine old material reworked or combined later, rather than either wholly authentic or wholly forged.')],
 ['wiki:The_Tale_of_Igor\'s_Campaign','wiki:Solar_eclipse_of_May_1,_1185'],img='File:Slovo o polku igoreve 1800.djvu',statusNote='Mainstream accepts authenticity; minority forgery view persists',dateNote='Events of 1185; manuscript found c. 1795, burned 1812'),
X('prague-orloj','The Prague Orloj and the legend of the blinded clockmaker',H,'confirmed',2,1410,['textual','material'],
 "The Prague astronomical clock (Orloj) on the Old Town Hall is the oldest astronomical clock still working. Its mechanical clock and astronomical dial were made in 1410 by the clockmaker Mikuláš of Kadaň and Jan Šindel, professor of mathematics and astronomy at Charles University; the first written record is dated 9 October 1410. The dial shows the positions of the Sun and Moon in the zodiac and several systems of time, and its moving figures were added later: wooden statues in the 17th century and the Apostles after a repair in 1787-1791. It has been repaired many times, including after heavy damage during the Prague uprising in May 1945.",
 [('The blinded master Hanuš','A legend retold by Alois Jirásek says the city councillors blinded the master Hanuš so he could never build a finer clock elsewhere, and that he took revenge by stopping it. Records show the clock was built in 1410, not by Hanuš (Jan Růže, active c. 1490), so historians regard the tale as legend; similar "blinded architect" tales are told of St Basil\'s Cathedral in Moscow and other buildings.'),
  ('Curse of the clock','Local tradition says Prague will suffer if the clock is neglected; this is folklore.'),
  ('Myth of the date','For centuries it was wrongly believed to date from 1490; the earlier date was established from archival documents.')],
 ['wiki:Prague_astronomical_clock'],img='Prague astronomical clock',
 changed='Until the 20th century, credited to Hanuš c. 1490; archival work established its creation in 1410 by Mikuláš of Kadaň and Jan Šindel.'),
X('jan-hus','Jan Hus: burned at Constance, and the "goose and swan" prophecy',H,'confirmed',2,1415,['textual'],
 "Jan Hus (c. 1369-1415) was a Czech priest, preacher at Prague's Bethlehem Chapel and rector of Charles University, who criticised clerical wealth, the sale of indulgences and papal authority, influenced by the English reformer John Wycliffe. Summoned to the Council of Constance with a promise of safe conduct from King Sigismund, he was arrested, tried for heresy and burned at the stake on 6 July 1415. His death sparked the Hussite movement and, after the First Defenestration of Prague in 1419, the Hussite Wars (1419-1434), in which Hussite armies under Jan Žižka repeatedly defeated crusades sent against them. He is seen as a forerunner of the Protestant Reformation.",
 [('The goose and the swan','A well-known tradition says Hus ("goose" in Czech) predicted that though they roast a goose now, in a hundred years a swan would come whom they could not silence; Lutherans later applied this to Martin Luther. Historians regard the precise saying as a later legend.'),
  ('Martyr or heretic','Catholic and Protestant traditions long judged him very differently; in 1999 Pope John Paul II expressed "deep regret" for his cruel death, without formally rehabilitating his teachings.'),
  ('Broken promise','Whether Sigismund\'s safe conduct was betrayed or legally void for a heretic was argued at the time and later; it fuelled Czech anger.')],
 ['wiki:Jan_Hus','wiki:Hussite_Wars'],img='Jan Hus',dateNote='Burned 6 July 1415; Hussite Wars 1419-1434'),
X('onfim-birch-bark','Onfim and the birch-bark letters of Novgorod',H,'confirmed',2,1260,['archaeological','textual'],
 "On 26 July 1951, during excavations in Novgorod, Russia, Nina Akulova found the first medieval letter scratched on birch bark. Over 1,100 have since been found in Novgorod (and more in other Russian towns), dating from the 11th to the 15th centuries, preserved in up to 8 m of waterlogged clay. Most are everyday messages in the local Old Novgorodian dialect: business deals, debts, family news, love letters, complaints and schoolwork. Among the most famous are around 17 pieces left by a boy called Onfim in the mid-13th century, about 6-7 years old, with alphabet exercises and drawings of himself as a mounted warrior spearing enemies and as a 'wild beast'.",
 [('Literacy rethink','Before 1951 most scholars assumed medieval Rus\' was almost entirely illiterate outside the clergy and elite; the letters show writing was used by merchants, artisans, women and children.'),
  ('Untapped archive','Less than 3% of medieval Novgorod has been excavated, and the expedition leader Valentin Yanin estimated that more than 20,000 letters may remain underground.'),
  ('Language evidence','The letters revealed a distinct dialect and helped settle debates such as the authenticity of the Tale of Igor\'s Campaign; one letter (no. 292) is the oldest known text in a Finnic language.')],
 ['wiki:Birch_bark_manuscript','wiki:Onfim'],img='Onfim',dateNote='Letters 11th-15th century; Onfim c. 1260; first found 1951',
 changed='Pre-1951: medieval Rus\' assumed largely illiterate; after 1951: birch-bark letters revealed widespread everyday literacy.'),
X('turul','The Turul: the falcon that fathered Hungary\'s first dynasty',M,'myth-legend',3,819,['textual','folklore'],
 "The turul is a mythical bird of prey, usually a falcon, in Hungarian tradition. The Gesta Hungarorum (c. 1200) and the Illuminated Chronicle tell how Emese, pregnant (or made pregnant by the bird), dreamed that a turul came to her and that a torrent flowed from her womb, foretelling that glorious kings would descend from her son Álmos (named from álom, 'dream'), father of Árpád, who led the Hungarian conquest of the Carpathian Basin around 895. The Árpád dynasty, which ruled until 1301, was called the 'Turul clan'. The turul became a national symbol in the 19th century and appears on monuments, at Buda Castle and in the emblem of the Hungarian Defence Forces.",
 [('Steppe shamanic roots','Scholars place the story in a widespread Eurasian steppe tradition of totemic birds and divine ancestry, with parallels in Turkic and Mongol legends, such as a dream of a white falcon in The Secret History of the Mongols.'),
  ('Chronicle invention?','Some historians emphasise that the chronicles were written 300 years after the conquest and shaped the legend to glorify the royal house; how much is genuine early tradition is debated.'),
  ('Contested symbol','In modern times the turul has been used by different political movements, including far-right groups, making it a debated emblem.')],
 ['wiki:Turul','wiki:Árpád_dynasty'],img='Turul',dateNote='Chronicles give 819 for Álmos\'s birth; written down c. 1200'),
]
